Task 3: Ask badly






What you should find





What comes back varies, but it usually fails items 1, 3 and 10 to 12 of the table standard: raw column names such as state_name and corn_yield, several decimals, and no title or notes. It also has to guess things only you decided:

what “drought” means: any week of extreme drought, a week or more, or one of the milder categories;
whether to show averages, totals or something else;
the layout: which comparison goes down a column;
whether to show the number of county-years.

If your AI used weeks_extreme &gt; 0 as the definition, notice that 73 county-years have more than 0 but less than 1 week of extreme drought. Two reasonable definitions give two different tables, and only a note says which one the reader is looking at.

Checks that come from knowing the data
The checks above apply to any join. What you know about the data gives stronger ones.

A feature you know. Pick one well, look up its county on a map, and ask for that well’s row.
A share that must be whole. For watersheds and counties, each watershed’s pieces should cover the whole watershed. If they do not, part of it lies outside the area your county data covers.



Code the AI wrote
coverage &lt;- st_intersection(huc, nitrogen) |&gt;
  mutate(area = as.numeric(st_area(geom))) |&gt;
  st_drop_geometry() |&gt;
  group_by(HUC_CODE) |&gt;
  summarise(covered = sum(area)) |&gt;
  left_join(tibble(HUC_CODE = huc$HUC_CODE, total = as.numeric(st_area(huc))), by = "HUC_CODE")
cat("watersheds fully covered by county data: expected", nrow(huc), "got", sum(coverage$covered / coverage$total &gt; 0.99), "\n")


watersheds fully covered by county data: expected 28 got 24 


The check fails: 4 of the 28 watersheds are only partly covered, one of them only 29%, because they extend beyond Iowa and the nitrogen data covers Iowa counties only. An average nitrogen rate for those watersheds describes their Iowa part only. Whether that is acceptable is a decision about the research, not the code; the point is that you now know to make it.
